<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sephardic Jews</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sephardim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sephardic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> are the Jewish communities descended from those of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal, expelled in 1492 and 1497. Many settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and from there in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guianas">Guianas</a>. They brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and trading expertise to colonies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> and Curaçao, where Mikvé Israel-Emanuel is the oldest surviving synagogue in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews.jpg" alt="Sephardic Jews" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfred Dehodencq · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F%C3%AAte_juive_%C3%A0_T%C3%A9touan,_Alfred_Dehodencq,_1865.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Sephardic comes from Sepharad, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> word for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. The earliest significant Jewish presence in the Iberian Peninsula is traced to the Roman period, in the first centuries CE. Around 300 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-elvira">Synod of Elvira</a> enacted decrees restricting contact between Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, among them bans on intermarriage and communal dining. Jewish communities faced persecution under Visigothic rule and largely welcomed the Muslim conquest that began in 711. Under Islamic rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a>, Jews were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a>, protected but second-class monotheists, who practised their religion with relative autonomy in return for a special tax.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a> in 1085, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from administrative posts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, and by the 13th century Jewish life had largely shifted to Christian territories. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a> patronised scholars of many backgrounds, including Jews, but the legal code compiled in his reign, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siete-partidas">Siete Partidas</a>, imposed restrictions on Jews, among them distinguishing clothing and residential segregation. In the summer of 1391 anti-Jewish riots began in Seville and spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Aragon. Jewish quarters were looted, synagogues destroyed, thousands killed and thousands more forcibly baptised, and the Jewish communities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> were wiped out.</p><p>On 31 March 1492 the Catholic Monarchs issued the Alhambra Decree, ordering practising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Aragon by 31 July, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-of-portugal">Manuel I of Portugal</a> issued a similar edict. Over 200,000 Jews converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, and between 40,000 and 100,000 were expelled. Many found refuge in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>'s Jewish population grew to around 40,000 between 1477 and 1535 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>'s, nonexistent in 1478, to over 16,500 by 1519. Others settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, in Italian ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. Many conversos stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, some secretly keeping Jewish practices; they were investigated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a> and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Portuguese Inquisition</a>, established in 1536.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Sephardic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> spoke variants of Spanish, Catalan or Portuguese. Ladino or Judaeo-Spanish, also called Judezmo, derives from Old Spanish and Old Portuguese, with additions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and local languages, and was often written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashi">Rashi</a> script. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> the variety known as Haketia took on Arabic influences. Wherever Sephardim settled they founded schools that used Spanish. They are commonly divided into Eastern Sephardim in the Ottoman lands, North African Sephardim, and Western Sephardim, descendants of conversos, who in the 17th century were prominent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. They reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the mid-17th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sephardic Jews” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Dutch Reformed Church was the public church, but Catholics, Mennonites, Lutherans and Jews could worship privately, and <b>Sephardic Jews</b> from Portugal settled in Amsterdam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch and Flemish refugees, Portuguese <b>Sephardic Jews</b> and, from 1567, the English Merchant Adventurers made Hamburg a centre of the cloth, sugar and Iberian trades.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>This pluralism made it easier to absorb diverse skilled populations, among them the <b>Sephardic Jews</b> expelled from Spain in 1492, who brought commercial networks and technical expertise such as printing to Ottoman cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A notable <b>Sephardic Jewish</b> community, with its own self-governing settlement at Jodensavanne on the Suriname River, owned many plantations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Amsterdam welcomed the <b>Sephardic Jews</b> and granted them all privileges except citizenship, but its tolerance served the city’s convenience: poor Ashkenazi Jews from Eastern Europe were far more carefully vetted, and those who came to depend on the city were encouraged to move on.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sephardim</b> and British synagogues sing it at the close of the Shabbat and festival morning service, many communities at the end of Mussaf, the German rite at the start of daily morning prayers, and Ashkenazim sometimes in place of Yigdal at the close of the evening service.</blockquote><a href="/songs/adon-olam">Adon Olam</a></li></ul></section>
